  "worked_solution": "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=7$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)} & ={\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)}|}_{x=7} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(7)}{{7}^{2}\\cdot g(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{49\\cdot 0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{(f(x))^{′}}{({x}^{2}g(x))^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2xg(x)+{x}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=7$ again, and this time, we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2xg(x)+{x}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)} & ={\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)}{2xg(x)+{x}^{2}{g}^{′}(x)}|}_{x=7} \\\\  & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(7)}{2\\cdot 7\\cdot g(7)+{7}^{2}\\cdot {g}^{′}(7)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+49\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{98}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to 7}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}g(x)}=\\frac{1}{98}.$",
